        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-serif text-navy-900 mb-4 leading-tight">
          Car scrappage value India: RVSF rates, policy rules & certificate of deposit (2026)
        </h1>
        <Byline authorSlug="s-iyer" publishedDate="30 July 2026" readTime="12 min read" />
      </div>

      <div class="prose-content space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-graphite">

        <div class="card-institutional bg-cream-100 border-l-4 border-signal-600 text-sm text-graphite">
          <strong class="text-navy-900">Editorial explainer — not policy or tax advice.</strong> This article summarises publicly available scrappage rules and RVSF concession schemes for general information. TheResaleValue is not affiliated with MoRTH, any state government, any RVSF, or any OEM. All payout figures, tax rebates, and timelines are illustrative and change frequently at the state level. Please check the official MoRTH Vehicle Scrappage Policy notification and your state transport department for current rules before scrapping any vehicle.
        </div>

        <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
          A 2008 Maruti Swift in Delhi. The local kabadiwala offers ₹18,000, cash, same day. The owner hesitates, the car barely runs, the RC renewal is pending, and the 15-year mark is approaching. A visit to a Mahindra CERO facility tells a different story: ₹8,000 to ₹12,000 in scrap metal payout, but a Certificate of Deposit that unlocks a 25% road tax rebate and full registration fee waiver on a new car. When the numbers are totalled, metal payout plus road tax savings plus registration waiver, the RVSF route yields ₹45,000 to ₹65,000 in effective value. The kabadiwala's ₹18,000 suddenly looks thin.
        </p>

        <p>
          That gap between perceived value and actual value is where most owners lose money. This page breaks down what a car is worth at end of life in India in 2026, how the scrappage policy works in practice, and where the real savings sit.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">How much do you get for scrapping a car in India?</h2>

        <p>
          <strong class="text-navy-900">An RVSF in India typically pays ₹5,000 to ₹15,000 for scrapping a hatchback and ₹15,000 to ₹35,000 for an SUV,</strong> based on the car's kerb weight and current scrap metal commodity prices. The total economic value is higher because owners also receive a Certificate of Deposit that unlocks road tax rebates (up to 25%) and registration fee waivers on a new car purchase. The metal payout alone never tells the full story.
        </p>

        <p>
          Here is what actually happens at the facility. The RVSF weighs the car on an industrial scale, then strips it in a specific sequence. Hazardous materials come out first: battery, engine oil, coolant, brake fluid, AC refrigerant. The battery is valued separately (a lead-acid car battery fetches ₹400 to ₹800 depending on weight and lead prices). Catalytic converters, where present, are priced independently too. They contain platinum-group metals and can add ₹1,500 to ₹5,000 to the payout depending on the converter type and condition. After stripping, the remaining shell is weighed for recoverable metal: steel, aluminium, copper wiring, plastic components.
        </p>

        <p><strong class="text-navy-900">RVSF scrap value ranges by vehicle type (estimated, 2026):</strong></p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
          <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead class="bg-cream-100">
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left p-2 border border-cream-200 font-semibold text-navy-900">Vehicle type</th>
