              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Hatchback (Alto, i10)</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">700 to 1,000 kg</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">₹8,000 to ₹18,000</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">₹5,000 to ₹12,000</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Sedan (City, Ciaz)</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">1,000 to 1,300 kg</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">₹14,000 to ₹25,000</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">₹9,000 to ₹18,000</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">SUV (Innova, Scorpio)</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">1,500 to 2,000 kg</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">₹22,000 to ₹40,000</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">₹15,000 to ₹35,000</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Two-wheeler</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">100 to 180 kg</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">₹1,500 to ₹3,500</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">₹800 to ₹2,500</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Light commercial vehicle</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">1,800 to 3,500 kg</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">₹30,000 to ₹65,000</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">₹20,000 to ₹50,000</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          Payouts shift month to month. Scrap steel prices in India have fluctuated between ₹22 to ₹32 per kg over the past two years. A 2009 Hyundai i10 (kerb weight approximately 950 kg) scrapped in a month when steel trades at ₹28 per kg yields roughly ₹10,500 in recoverable metal value after RVSF margin deduction. That same car scrapped three months later with steel at ₹24 per kg drops to around ₹8,500. Timing matters.
        </p>

        <p>
          Most people think the metal payout is the only number that matters. That is only half the story. The part most owners overlook is the <a href="/blogs/idv-calculator-india">Insured Declared Value</a> of a 15-year-old car. At that age, IDV is functionally zero. Insurance companies will not write a meaningful policy. The car's value exists only in its metal, its Certificate of Deposit, and the tax concessions that come with formal scrappage.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">RVSF vs local scrap dealer: price gap, process gap, and the legal risk no one mentions</h2>

        <p>
          A Registered Vehicle Scrapping Facility (RVSF) is a government-authorised centre licensed to dismantle end-of-life cars, issue Certificates of Deposit, and handle environmental disposal under CPCB norms. A local scrap dealer, the neighbourhood kabadiwala, is none of these things.
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
          <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead class="bg-cream-100">
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left p-2 border border-cream-200 font-semibold text-navy-900">Factor</th>
                <th class="text-left p-2 border border-cream-200 font-semibold text-navy-900">RVSF</th>
                <th class="text-left p-2 border border-cream-200 font-semibold text-navy-900">Local scrap dealer</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Cash payout (hatchback)</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">₹5,000 to ₹12,000</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">₹12,000 to ₹22,000</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Cash payout (SUV)</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">₹15,000 to ₹35,000</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">₹25,000 to ₹45,000</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Certificate of Deposit</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Yes</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">No</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Road tax rebate on new car</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Yes (up to 25%)</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Not applicable</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Registration fee waiver</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Yes</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Not applicable</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">De-registration handled</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Yes, RVSF initiates with RTO</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">No, owner must do it separately</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Environmental compliance</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">CPCB norms followed</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">No oversight</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Legal liability after sale</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Extinguished</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Remains with owner until RC is cancelled</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          The local dealer's cash number is higher. That is the draw. But the total value calculation flips once Certificate of Deposit concessions are factored in. And the legal risk of selling to an unregistered dealer is real. The car's chassis number plate and engine number remain linked to the original owner's RC until formal de-registration happens. If that chassis number shows up in a stolen-car recovery or an accident investigation, the paperwork trail leads straight back to the original owner.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to find an RVSF near you</h3>

        <p>
          Visit vscrap.parivahan.gov.in, select the state, and view the list of registered facilities with addresses and contact numbers. Each listing shows the facility's registration number and operational status.
        </p>

        <p>
          Not every district has an RVSF. If the nearest facility is 100 kilometres or more away, transport costs eat into the payout. Some RVSFs offer pickup services for non-running cars; others do not. Towing a non-running car 80 kilometres can cost ₹3,000 to ₹6,000, which wipes out the metal payout for a small hatchback entirely.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">India vehicle scrappage policy 2026: which cars must be scrapped and which can re-register</h2>

        <p>
          The scrappage policy does not force private car owners to scrap outright. It requires cars older than 15 years (petrol) or 10 years (diesel, in specific states and cities) to pass a fitness test at an Automated Testing Station (ATS) before re-registration is permitted. Cars that fail the ATS test face mandatory de-registration, and at that point, scrappage through an RVSF becomes the only formal option. This framework flows from MoRTH notification G.S.R. 653(E) dated 2021 and subsequent amendments that tightened the diesel car timeline.
        </p>

        <p>
          Government-owned vehicles 15 years or older are already under mandatory scrappage. That rule has been active since April 2022. For private owners, the policy is effectively mandatory only when the car cannot pass fitness.
        </p>

        <p>
          State-by-state enforcement varies sharply. Delhi NCR enforces the 10-year diesel rule and 15-year petrol rule actively, with the National Green Tribunal backing enforcement orders. Gujarat and Maharashtra have operational ATS centres and are issuing fitness test notices. Several other states have ATS centres approved on paper but not yet functional on the ground.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">The re-registration cost trap: when it makes more sense to scrap</h3>

        <p>
          Take a 2010 Maruti Alto in Delhi, petrol, 16 years old. Re-registration requires: green tax (₹2,500 to ₹5,000 annually, varies by state), ATS fitness test fee (₹300 to ₹500), increased insurance premium for an aged car (if an insurer agrees to cover it at all), and the re-registration fee itself (₹3,000 to ₹5,000 for a car this age in Delhi).
        </p>

        <p>
          The re-registration route costs ₹8,000 to ₹15,000 upfront, plus recurring green tax every year. The car's private resale value sits at ₹25,000 to ₹40,000 at best. Now compare: scrapping at an RVSF yields ₹5,000 to ₹10,000 in metal payout plus a Certificate of Deposit worth ₹15,000 to ₹40,000 in concessions on a new car. If a new purchase is planned, scrappage wins. If the owner intends to keep driving the car for 2 to 3 more years and has no plans to buy new, re-registration might make sense, but only if the car passes fitness.
        </p>

        <p>
          Here is the break-even logic: when the car's remaining private-sale value exceeds the total scrappage benefit (metal plus CoD plus tax concessions), selling privately is rational. For a 2010 Alto, scrappage almost always wins. For a 2010 Honda City in decent condition with a private resale value of ₹1,20,000, the private sale wins.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Certificate of Deposit: what it's worth and how long it lasts</h2>

        <p>
          The Certificate of Deposit (CoD) is a formal document issued by the RVSF after a car is fully scrapped and dismantled. It is legal proof of end-of-life disposal and the document that unlocks road tax rebates and registration fee waivers when buying a new car. The CoD is issued by the RVSF, not the RTO. Owners do not need to visit the RTO to obtain it.
        </p>

        <p>The CoD contains: original vehicle registration number, chassis number, engine number, date of scrapping, RVSF name and registration number, owner details, and a unique certificate number linked to the VAHAN database.</p>

        <p>
          That changes the timeline. Without a CoD, none of the government concessions are accessible. A local scrap dealer cannot issue one. Only a registered RVSF can.
        </p>

        <p>
          One detail worth flagging: the CoD may or may not be transferable to a family member. Some interpretations of MoRTH rules allow immediate family members (spouse, children) to use the CoD when purchasing a new car. Others restrict it to the registered owner of the scrapped car. Clarity on this point varies by state and by the RTO officer processing the registration.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Tax concessions and registration discounts: state-wise breakdown for 2026</h2>

        <p>
          The central government guideline under the Vehicle Scrappage Policy offers up to 25% rebate on road tax for non-transport cars and up to 15% for transport cars when a Certificate of Deposit is presented during new car registration, as per MoRTH's notification on scrappage incentives. The registration fee for the new car is fully waived in most states that have adopted the policy.
        </p>

        <p>The actual benefit depends entirely on which state the new car is registered in. Some states have adopted the full central concession. Others offer reduced rates. A few have not formally notified any concession yet.</p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
          <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead class="bg-cream-100">
