              <tr><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Gujarat</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Up to 25%</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Full waiver</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Uttar Pradesh</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Up to 15%</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Full waiver</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Telangana</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Up to 25%</td><td class="p-2 border border-cream-200">Full waiver</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          Some OEMs offer additional exchange bonuses when a CoD is presented. Maruti Suzuki, Tata Motors, and Hyundai have run scrappage exchange programs at various points. Check current offers at the dealership level, as these change quarterly.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Scrapping a 2009 Honda City in Maharashtra and buying a new Tata Nexon</h3>

        <p>A worked example. A 2009 Honda City (kerb weight approximately 1,100 kg) scrapped at an RVSF in Maharashtra:</p>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li>Metal scrap payout: ₹10,000 to ₹16,000</li>
          <li>Certificate of Deposit: issued (no direct cash value, but enables concessions below)</li>
          <li>Road tax rebate on new Tata Nexon (ex-showroom approximately ₹10 lakh): 25% of Maharashtra road tax. Maharashtra road tax on a Nexon is approximately ₹1 lakh, so the rebate comes to approximately ₹25,000</li>
          <li>Registration fee waiver: approximately ₹6,000 saved</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Total effective value of scrapping: ₹41,000 to ₹47,000</strong></li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          Now compare to private sale. A 2009 Honda City in running condition sells privately for ₹80,000 to ₹1,20,000 in most cities. If the owner can find a buyer at ₹1 lakh or more, private sale wins. If the car is not running, has failed fitness, or cannot find a buyer, the RVSF route delivers more total value than a local kabadiwala's ₹20,000 cash offer.
        </p>

        <p>
          Check the car's private market worth on the <a href="/valuation">used car valuation calculator</a> before making the call.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">The scrappage process step by step</h2>

        <p>The scrappage process in India follows a seven-step sequence from the moment a car fails its ATS fitness test (or the owner voluntarily decides to scrap) to the final bank credit. The entire process takes 7 to 15 business days in most cases, though the RVSF-to-RTO de-registration step can stretch longer in states with manual processing.</p>

        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Car fails ATS fitness test or owner decides to scrap voluntarily.</strong> No fitness failure is required for voluntary scrappage. An owner can walk into an RVSF with a running car and request scrapping.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Book a slot at an RVSF.</strong> Use vscrap.parivahan.gov.in to find a registered facility. Some RVSFs accept walk-ins; others require advance booking, especially in Delhi NCR where demand is higher.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Deliver the car to the RVSF with documents.</strong> Required: original Registration Certificate (RC), valid ID proof (Aadhaar or PAN), address proof, insurance policy (even if lapsed), PUC certificate if available. If the car has an active loan or hypothecation noted on the RC, a No Objection Certificate (NOC) from the financing bank is mandatory.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">RVSF inspection, hazardous material removal, and valuation.</strong> The facility removes battery, fluids, AC refrigerant, and fuel first. The car is weighed. Recoverable components are catalogued. Typically takes 1 to 3 days.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Car scrapped, Certificate of Deposit issued.</strong> Once dismantling is complete, the RVSF generates a Certificate of Deposit through the VAHAN-linked system.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">RVSF initiates de-registration with the RTO.</strong> In most states, the RVSF sends the de-registration request directly to the jurisdictional RTO through the VAHAN system. The owner does not need to visit the RTO separately.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Scrap value payout credited to the owner's bank account.</strong> Payment is made via NEFT or RTGS to the bank account linked to the owner's KYC. Timeline: 3 to 7 business days after CoD issuance.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Old car scrap value: how Delhi, Mumbai, and Bangalore differ</h2>

        <p>Scrappage value is not uniform across India. Three factors create city-level variation: the number of operational RVSFs (more facilities means more competition and slightly better payouts), local scrap metal commodity rates (steel prices vary regionally by ₹2 to ₹4 per kg), and state-specific tax concessions.</p>

        <p>
          <strong class="text-navy-900">Delhi NCR:</strong> Strictest enforcement in the country. The National Green Tribunal's diesel car ban (10 years) and petrol car limit (15 years) are actively enforced. Delhi has the highest concentration of operational RVSFs, including Mahindra CERO's facility. Competition among facilities can push metal payouts slightly higher. Typical hatchback scrap value range: ₹6,000 to ₹13,000 metal payout, with CoD concessions adding ₹20,000 to ₹35,000 when buying a new car in Delhi.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong class="text-navy-900">Mumbai / Maharashtra:</strong> Maharashtra offers the full 25% road tax rebate and registration fee waiver. Mumbai has fewer RVSFs than Delhi, which means less competition but also longer wait times for appointments. Transport costs to an RVSF outside Mumbai city can add ₹2,000 to ₹5,000.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong class="text-navy-900">Bangalore / Karnataka:</strong> Karnataka has adopted the central concession framework. RVSF availability in Bangalore is growing but still limited compared to Delhi. The practical challenge here is that many 15-year-old cars in Bangalore are still in running condition due to better road conditions and climate, making private sale a more viable alternative than scrappage.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Frequently asked questions</h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">How much money do I get for scrapping my car at an RVSF in India?</h3>
        <p>
          The RVSF metal payout for a hatchback ranges from ₹5,000 to ₹15,000, and for an SUV from ₹15,000 to ₹35,000, depending on kerb weight and current scrap metal prices. The total economic value is higher when Certificate of Deposit concessions are added. Road tax rebate (up to 25%) and registration fee waiver on a new car can add ₹15,000 to ₹40,000 in effective savings.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Is the vehicle scrappage policy mandatory in India in 2026?</h3>
        <p>
          Not for all private cars. Private cars that fail the ATS fitness test cannot be re-registered, which makes scrappage the only formal option. Government vehicles 15 years or older are already under mandatory scrappage since April 2022, as per MoRTH notification G.S.R. 653(E). For private owners whose cars pass the fitness test, scrappage remains voluntary.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">What is a certificate of deposit in vehicle scrappage?</h3>
        <p>
          A Certificate of Deposit (CoD) is a formal document issued by a Registered Vehicle Scrapping Facility after a car has been fully dismantled. It proves that the car was disposed of through authorised channels and entitles the owner to road tax rebates and registration fee waivers when purchasing a new car. The CoD is generated digitally through the VAHAN system and carries a unique certificate number.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Can I scrap my car without visiting the RTO?</h3>
        <p>
          In most states, yes. The RVSF handles de-registration directly with the RTO through the VAHAN database. The owner's responsibility is limited to delivering the car and documents to the RVSF and receiving the Certificate of Deposit and payout. Some states may still require an owner visit for final de-registration, though this is gradually being phased out.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">What is the difference between an RVSF and a local scrap dealer?</h3>
        <p>
          An RVSF is a government-registered facility authorised to dismantle cars, issue Certificates of Deposit, and manage environmental disposal under CPCB guidelines. A local scrap dealer is unregistered, cannot issue a CoD, does not handle de-registration, and offers no legal closure on the car's identity. The RVSF payout is typically 15 to 30% lower in cash, but the total value (including CoD concessions) usually exceeds the local dealer's offer.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">How much road tax refund do I get when I scrap my old car and buy a new one?</h3>
        <p>
          The central government guideline provides up to 25% rebate on road tax for non-transport cars and up to 15% for transport cars. The actual percentage depends on the state where the new car is registered. The rebate is applied only when a valid Certificate of Deposit is presented during new car registration. State-specific rates range from 15% to 25%.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">What documents do I need to scrap my car at an RVSF?</h3>
        <p>
          Required: original Registration Certificate (RC), valid government ID (Aadhaar, PAN, or passport), address proof, insurance policy (even if expired), and PUC certificate if available. If the car has a loan or hypothecation recorded on the RC, a No Objection Certificate (NOC) from the financing bank is mandatory before the RVSF will accept the car.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Is it better to sell my old car privately or scrap it at an RVSF?</h3>
        <p>
          It depends on the car's condition and residual resale value. For most 15-plus year old hatchbacks (Maruti Alto, Hyundai Santro, Maruti WagonR), the total scrappage benefit, metal payout plus CoD concessions, exceeds what a private buyer would pay. For well-maintained sedans and premium models (Honda City, Hyundai Verna, Toyota Corolla) with private resale values above ₹80,000 to ₹1 lakh, selling privately typically yields more. Run both calculations and compare against a <a href="/valuation">used car valuation</a> to make the call.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">What happens to my car after it is scrapped at an RVSF?</h3>
        <p>
          The RVSF first removes all hazardous materials: battery acid, engine oil, coolant, brake fluid, and AC refrigerant, following Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB) disposal guidelines. Reusable parts that meet safety norms (headlights, mirrors, starter motors) are catalogued and may be resold. The metal body is shredded and sold as scrap to steel and aluminium recyclers. Plastic components are separated and sent to authorised plastic recycling facilities. The entire process is logged and reported to MoRTH through the VAHAN-linked system.
        </p>

